Actually, if something does damage which ends up with a total of 0 (or less) then no damage is taken, thus it doesn&#39;t qualify as next damage (thus an attack of 0 will not remove stealth protection)However, irreducible damage would use up the protection, with none of the benifets of the protection.
</blockquote></div>
That seems wrong to me. Wouldn&#39;t that make it possible for Wraith to become completely unkillable against certain villains without irreducible damage?

[/quote]

&nbsp;

Not sure how you reached that conclusion. I think JayMann is saying that if an attack is reduced to zero damage by something OTHER than the stealth power, than the stealth reduction doesn&#39;t kick in.

&nbsp;

Mind you, assuming I&#39;m correct about what JayMann wrote, I disagree. Even if someone tried to somehow attack The Wraith with a negative 50 million attack, her Stealth power kicks in on the next attack. Even if she&#39;s immune, that reduction kicks in on the next attack.
